      },
      {
        "text": "Marginal cost represents a rational benchmark for sustainable pricing.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Retailers and manufacturers will cease operations when profitability drops below marginal cost.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The cited expert opinion carries significant credibility and weight on economic policy matters.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Economic outcomes like supply shortages are undesirable consequences of price controls.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Correction / Challenge"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 51298,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51298.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that you're seeing very high profit levels for a number of corporations, for food, for housing, for gasoline. And so they, for drug prices, good grief, look at how inexpensively they make insulin and yet how much they charge for it. So if you bring those prices down, they're not necessarily going to be below market value. And they can still make a tremendous amount of profit. We've negotiated those prices and you've gone from hundreds of dollars at their charge and to tens of dollars and they're still willing to make that deal.",
