    "turn_text": "Our headlines this hour, Hezbollah, is reported to have fired more than 100 rockets from Lebanon into Israel in a single day as hopes fade that U.S. ceasefire efforts will succeed. Studies found that Brazil has lost a third of its natural areas since records began and scientists working on India's lunar exploration say they have evidence that an ocean of molten rock once covered moon's south pole. This is Sean Lay with NewsHour, live from the BBC in London. India's space scientists say the landing on the moon of the lunar module Vikram a year ago is provided evidence to support a theory about how it was formed 4.5 billion years ago. They believe the surface was once covered by an ocean of magma, liquid molten rock at the South Pole. They've also found evidence of an asteroid strike four billion years ago. Well, Professor Anal Bhardwaj is Director of the Physical Research Laboratory in the Metabat, which is part of the government's space department.",
