    "turn_text": "The Irish Women's Liberation Movement, which was set up by Nell and many other of her contemporaries, were really influenced by the type of direct-action activism you saw with the civil rights movements and the feminist movements in the 1960s. But of course, the 60s didn't hit Ireland until the 70s. So they produced a manifesto, but they really wanted to make an impact. And the idea, the ridiculous idea, that contraceptives were totally banned in the Republic of Ireland, but available in Northern Ireland, really appealed to their sense of injustice. So the idea was to take the train from Dublin to Belfast by condoms and the pill and all that sort of thing and bring it back. And of course in Connolly station in Dublin, because they were coming from what was then considered a cross-border, the customs officials should stop them, search them and then they would have illegal contraband. And this would create a big fuss. So they all gathered in Connolly station took the train to Belfast, not realizing, of course, that you needed a prescription to get the pill. So apparently they didn't actually get the pill, they got desperate, or some other type of pill, but they did get the condoms, bringing them back. And of course, they had arranged that all of the other group with a big banner, and there's a very iconic photo of the marching up Connolly station platform, On the return from Belfast and and the customs officials and and the guard the Irish police didn't really know what to do with these crazy women these young crazy feminists so they weren't arrested and mainly they blew up the continent the condoms like balloons",
